1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,100
Listen to a talk in a science class today.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:11,130
I want to tell you about an unusual phenomenon that scientists noticed in the Pacific Ocean in the early 1990's.

3
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:17,030
The scientists were recording sounds using hydrophones positioned on the ocean floor in the North Pacific.

4
00:00:17,030 --> 00:00:22,160
Hydrophones are specially designed microphones that allow scientists to collect acoustic data underwater.

5
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:27,513
While most of the undersea noises picked up by the hydrophones emanated from whales, ships' engines,

6
00:00:27,513 --> 00:00:33,200
or earthquakes, the scientists monitoring the hydrophones detected a number of sounds that they could not identify.

7
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:41,000
One was a regular series of rising infrasonic tones that have very short wavelengths below those humans can hear.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,040
The scientists labeled these very low frequency noises "upsweep."

9
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:49,110
At first, these sounds were thought to be the songs of whales migrating in the Pacific.

10
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:53,560
However, scientists who study whale acoustics eventually ruled out this possibility,

11
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:57,200
and the origin of the upsweep sounds remained unknown.

12
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:01,190
So what is the source of the rising tones detected by the hydrophones?

13
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:05,882
The sounds were ultimately tracked to an area of inferred volcanic seismic activity -

14
00:01:05,882 --> 00:01:09,130
a chain of undersea mountains in the South Pacific.

15
00:01:09,130 --> 00:01:13,138
Some scientists believe that if these mountains contain an active volcano,

16
00:01:13,138 --> 00:01:20,060
the source for the mysterious upsweep sounds may be hot volcanic lava coming in contact with cold sea water.
